   "text": "Clarke & Brown's Yarra Yarra Mining Company began a canal in December 1859 at Thompson's Creek with about 25 men; the canal was planned 50 ft wide at the top, 12 ft deep and 14 ft at the bottom. It used an 8-hp steam engine, a Start's patented puddling machine and an inclined tramway lifting wash 30 ft. It suspended in June 1860, restarted in November 1860 with two steam engines and was defunct by October 1861; small parties then got 'excellent results' until the mid 1860s.",
