   "duration_s": 1193,
   "view_count": 1968,
   "in_study_area": true,
   "place": "Andersons Creek near the Fourth Hill car park, Warrandyte State Park (inferred)",
   "summary": "A July sluicing session on Andersons Creek, inferred from the presenter's remarks about Andersons flowing and the fire track. He runs a 6-inch sluice with a rubber mat because mercury from the old batteries can wash out of other mats, and he had found mercury-coated gold in clay at the spot before. The ground is solid clay with ironstone gravel over decomposed bedrock with a mineralised quartz contact. Two runs give a small amount of reefy gold, one piece silvered by mercury, about 0.03-0.07 (unit not spoken). The creek rises after rain and floods the dig. His advice: gold drops where a bedrock bar pushes it against the bank and on the outside bend as well as the inside; dig the creek bed two or three shovels deep to clay, not the banks, and fill the hole in.",
